{
  "slug": "cantor-diagonalization-oq-06-oq-01",
  "title": "Explicit Diagonal Real Missing From Any Listed Enumeration",
  "phase": "COMPLETED",
  "status": "completed",
  "tier": "B",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\forall\\, f : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R},\\ \\exists\\, x \\in [0,1],\\ \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ x \\neq f(n),\\ \\text{where } x \\text{ is the explicit diagonal real whose } n\\text{-th digit differs from that of } f(n), \\text{ not deduced from } \\aleph_0 < \\#\\mathbb{R}.",
    "plain": "Given any sequence f(0), f(1), f(2), … of real numbers, explicitly construct a specific real x (as a definable function of f) and prove directly that x ≠ f(n) for every n, because x was built to disagree with f(n) at digit position n. This is Cantor's constructive 1891 diagonal argument, replacing the parent entry's abstract route through the cardinal inequality ℵ₀ < #ℝ.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The diagonal argument underlies Cantor's theorem, Turing's halting problem, Gödel's incompleteness, and Russell's paradox; a digit-level formalization exposes the reusable mechanism instead of hiding it in a cardinality lemma.",
      "A constructive witness (an actual real x, not just an existence claim) is strictly stronger than the non-constructive cardinal statement and matches how the argument is taught.",
      "It provides a reusable template for downstream diagonalizations in the gallery and beyond."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [],
    "open": [
      "An explicit, definable map diagonalReal : (ℕ → ℝ) → ℝ producing a witness with diagonalReal f ≠ f n for all n, proven by direct digit comparison rather than via cardinality.",
      "A clean digit-extraction and digit-comparison lemma usable for the disagreement step (n-th binary or decimal digit of a real in [0,1]), handling expansion non-uniqueness."
    ],
    "goal": "Define the witness x as an explicit function of the enumeration f (e.g. a bit sequence b n = if nthDigit (f n) n = 0 then 1 else 0 summed into [0,1]) and prove x ≠ f n for every n by exhibiting the differing digit, with no appeal to Cardinal.not_countable_real or #ℝ = 𝔠."
  },
